# ---------------- ch64 Stubb's Supper ----------------
d['ch64']['essay']="""I find this chapter hard, and I have come to think I am meant to. It is a comedy built on a very old and very ugly stage joke — the white officer badgering the old Black cook, who is written in a dialect Melville lays on with a trowel — and for two or three pages it plays exactly as that joke would have played in a New York theater in 1850. And yet everything true in the chapter is said by Fleece, and the joke keeps turning until it lands on Stubb. "I'm bressed if he ain't more of shark dan Massa Shark hisself" is the last word, and Melville hands it to the cook and calls it "sage." So the question I keep asking is not whether the chapter is racist — parts of it plainly are, in the way the whole culture it came out of was — but what Melville is doing with the material. My answer, which I hold with some confidence, is that he is staging the joke in order to have it condemn the man telling it.

Look at the architecture. The chapter is three meals of the same animal, stacked. On deck, one man eats a steak by the light of a lamp burning the same whale's oil — Ishmael will spend the whole next chapter, [[ch65|The Whale as a Dish]], worrying at that oddity. Below the waterline, thousands of sharks eat the carcass in the dark, with a sound like "the smacking of their lips" that keeps the crew awake. And in the middle, the long paragraph on sharks: sea-fights, and then slave ships, where the sharks trot alongside "in case a parcel is to be carried anywhere." That word *parcel* is the darkest thing in the chapter and it is placed, I think deliberately, one page before the scene of a white officer bullying a Black man born in transit on a ferry crossing the Roanoke. Melville does not draw the line. He puts the two things next to each other and lets the reader draw it or fail to.

Then the sermon. Fleece is roused at midnight to preach to the sharks because Stubb wants a joke, and what comes out is better theology than anything Father Mapple said in New Bedford. "All angel is not'ing more dan de shark well goberned" — there is no third nature, no clean creature, only appetite and the governing of it. Compare Mapple's two-stranded lesson in [[ch9:Delight is to him|The Sermon]], which is about obedience and the delight of standing forth; Fleece's is about digestion. And Fleece knows his audience cannot hear him. The hungry can't listen and the full "sink in de sea, go fast to sleep on de coral, and can't hear not'ing at all." That is a whole theory of why preaching fails, delivered by a ninety-year-old man in his nightshirt to a churning of fins, and Stubb, chewing, says he's "about of the same opinion." The benediction is a curse: fill your bellies till they burst, and then die. I read it as the truest blessing in the book because it wishes the sharks exactly what they will get — and it applies without alteration to the men above, and to the one pacing in the cabin.

Two smaller things. First, Ahab appears for a single paragraph, looks at the dead whale "vacantly" — no, the word is that the sight "reminded him that Moby Dick was yet to be slain" — and goes below. The first kill of the voyage is, to its captain, a subtraction of time; the whole comic banquet happens in the space his indifference leaves open, and you can feel the ship relax when he goes. Second, the moment where the comedy stops: Fleece says that when he dies he "won't go nowhere; but some bressed angel will come and fetch him," and Melville writes that he says it "slowly, changing his whole air and demeanor." For one sentence the chapter takes him completely seriously. Then Stubb has to turn the tongs into a joke about the main-top, and I notice the joke has to work very hard to recover. That effort is the tell.

What I take from the chapter, finally, is a rule for reading the rest of the book: watch who gets the last word. Melville's officers talk more; his outsiders — Fleece here, Queequeg in [[ch66|The Shark Massacre]] with his "one dam Ingin," Pip later — talk less and are righter. The next time Stubb is charming, and he is charming often, I try to remember him eating by the whale's light and telling a man three times his age to be civil to sharks."""
d['ch64']['annotations'].insert(1,{"quote":"Stubb was a high liver; he was somewhat intemperately fond of the whale as a flavorish thing to his palate.","note":"\"Intemperately\" is a loaded word aboard a ship whose owner's sister brought temperance ginger instead of grog — see the ginger scene in [[ch72:Ginger-jub|The Monkey-Rope]]. Stubb's appetite is presented as a small vice, and the whole chapter is about appetite: his, the sharks', and by the end of [[ch65]] yours."})
d['ch64']['annotations'].insert(-1,{"quote":"Don’t I always say that to be good, a whale-steak must be tough? There are those sharks now over the side, don’t you see they prefer it tough and rare?","note":"Stubb aligns his own taste with the sharks' as a joke, and the joke is the thesis: he wants what they want, rarer. Fleece will make the alignment explicit in the last line. Notice how much of the chapter's meaning Stubb says himself without hearing it."})
d['ch64']['annotations'].insert(-1,{"quote":"Now, look here, bred’ren, just try wonst to be cibil, a helping yourselbs from dat whale. Don’t be tearin’ de blubber out your neighbour’s mout, I say.","note":"The sermon's practical ethic: not abstinence, just don't take from your neighbour's mouth. It is the most modest moral proposal in the book and the sharks ignore it entirely. Ishmael's own version of this ethic comes in [[ch94:squeeze ourselves into each other|A Squeeze of the Hand]] — and he is just as unsure it can be kept."})
